 # ------------------------------------------------------------------ NATURE
 ("Nature", None, 1, None, None, [
  (W, "The Nature branch answers a question that precedes all the others: what kind of thing is Bitcoin? Chapter 1 gives two complementary answers. The first describes the characteristics of the currency, which is virtual, borderless, decentralized and robust. The second describes the architecture, which the chapter lists as four innovations brought together: a decentralized peer-to-peer network, a public transaction journal, a set of rules for validation and issuance, and a mechanism for reaching consensus [AH]."),
  (Y, "A student who knows only how to use a wallet knows what Bitcoin does and not why it is built as it is. The characteristics explain the goals, and the architecture explains how the goals are met. The sidebar on digital currencies before Bitcoin makes the link: earlier systems were centralized and easy to attack, so a decentralized digital currency was needed to be robust against intervention by antagonists, whether legitimate governments or criminal elements [AH]."),
  (E, "The characteristics appear in the opening paragraphs of the chapter, where Bitcoin is called arguably the perfect form of money for the internet because it is fast, secure and borderless, and where the currency is described as entirely virtual [AH]. The architecture appears in the list of what Bitcoin consists of, and each item of that list is then explained in the Network branch and the Foundations branch."),
  (H, "The branch has two sections. The Characteristic section contains four concepts, virtual, borderless, decentralized and robust, each of which names a property that the chapter attributes to the currency. The Architecture section contains three concepts that correspond to three of the four innovations in the chapter's list: the peer-to-peer protocol, the public journal and the consensus rule set. The fourth innovation, the proof-of-work mechanism, has its own concept in the Network branch."),
  (K, "A list of attractive adjectives is not an argument. Each characteristic is a claim that can be examined, and each depends on conditions: a currency is borderless only where people can reach the network, decentralized only while control stays spread out, and robust only against the threats it was designed for. The branch should be read as an account of design goals, with the evidence supplied by the other branches."),
 ], None),
 ("Characteristic", None, 2, "Nature", None, [
  (W, "The Characteristic section collects four properties that the chapter attributes to the bitcoin currency: it is virtual, it is borderless, it is decentralized, and it is robust. The first two come from the opening description of the currency, where Bitcoin is called arguably the perfect form of money for the internet because it is fast, secure and borderless, and the last two from the sidebar that explains why a decentralized digital currency was needed at all [AH]. The four are properties of the thing itself and not of any program that uses it, which is why they sit in their own branch rather than among the wallets."),
  (Y, "These properties distinguish Bitcoin from conventional money and from the earlier digital currencies that the chapter's sidebar describes. Conventional money exists in a physical and an electronic form at once and is controlled by central institutions; the earlier digital currencies were electronic but centralized, and the sidebar records that they were therefore easy to attack and that most were litigated out of existence or collapsed with the company behind them [AH]. Bitcoin combines a purely virtual existence with the absence of any central authority, and the chapter presents that combination, rather than either half of it, as the reason the currency suits the internet."),
  (H, "Each concept in the section is defined by the sentence of the chapter that introduces it and is then connected to the mechanism that makes it true, because a property without a mechanism is only a slogan. Virtual is shown by computing a balance from a list of transfers, which demonstrates that the coins need not exist anywhere as objects. Borderless, decentralized and robust are referred to the mechanisms of the other branches: the peer-to-peer protocol carries a payment without asking where its parties live, the consensus rules let every participant check issuance for himself, and proof of work makes the rewriting of history expensive. A learner who can name the mechanism for each property has understood the section."),
 ], None),
 ("Virtual", "Virtual", 3, "Characteristic", ("balances computed from transactions", "The bitcoin currency is entirely virtual: there are no physical coins or even individual digital coins, only transactions that imply the coins.", (_VIRT, "(70000, 35000)")), [
  (W, "To say that the bitcoin currency is virtual is to say that it has no physical or object form. The chapter states this precisely: unlike traditional currencies, the bitcoin currency is entirely virtual, there are no physical coins or even individual digital coins, and the coins are implied in transactions that transfer value from spender to receiver [AH]."),
  (Y, "The statement removes a common picture. Many people imagine a bitcoin as a file or a token that moves from one wallet to another, but nothing moves; the record of transfers is all there is. This is why the central question of the system is who may create a valid transfer, which the chapter answers by the control of keys: users control keys that allow them to prove ownership, and with these keys they sign transactions to unlock the value and spend it by transferring it to a new owner [AH]."),
  (E, "The property shows in every wallet. The balance that Alice sees is not a number stored somewhere as a coin; it is a figure that her wallet calculates from the transactions that involve her addresses. Alice's wallet shows 0.001 BTC as incoming as soon as it sees Joe's transaction, even though no coin has been delivered in the physical sense [AH]."),
  (H, "The example represents a tiny history as a list of transfers, Joe pays Alice 100000 satoshis, Alice pays Eve 30000, and Joe pays Eve 5000. The program computes each person's balance by adding what they received and subtracting what they sent: Alice has 70000 and Eve has 35000. No coin exists in the program, only the list. Bitcoin works on the same principle, with the list made public and validated by the nodes."),
  (K, "Virtual does not mean unreal. The value is real because people accept it, in the same way that the value of a bank balance is real. It also does not mean that nothing can be lost, since a lost key makes the funds unspendable. Finally, the word does not describe the system as a whole: the network, the miners and the wallets are physical machines that use real electricity and real hardware."),
 ]),
 ("Borderless", "Borderless", 3, "Characteristic", ("money for the internet", "Bitcoin is fast, secure and borderless, which the chapter gives as the reason that it is arguably the perfect form of money for the internet.", None), [
  (W, "Borderless means that the currency is not tied to any country or to its financial institutions. The chapter says that Bitcoin is arguably the perfect form of money for the internet because it is fast, secure and borderless [AH]. A payment is sent to an address and does not depend on the nationality of the recipient."),
  (Y, "Conventional payments between countries pass through banks and clearing systems that apply different rules and take time. The internet itself has no such borders, which is why a money that behaves like an internet service fits it so well. The chapter's list of what users can do is long: Bitcoin can be used to buy and sell goods, send money to people or organisations, or extend credit, just as with conventional currencies [AH]."),
  (E, "The property is met in remittances sent home across a border, in online purchases from a foreign seller, and in the global markets where bitcoin is exchanged for other currencies; the chapter notes that bitcoin can be purchased, sold and exchanged for other currencies at specialised currency exchanges [AH]. It is also met in the reach of the software itself, since the protocol stack is open source and runs on a wide range of devices, including laptops and smartphones, which the chapter gives as the reason the technology is easily accessible [AH]. A payment to an address therefore looks the same to the network whether the payee is in the next street or on another continent."),
  (H, "Bitcoin is borderless because the network is a peer-to-peer system on top of the internet and the rules are the same everywhere. Anyone can run the software on a laptop or a smartphone, as the chapter notes, and any participant can send to any address [AH]. Where bitcoin touches the traditional system, at exchanges for example, national and international regulations often apply."),
  (K, "Borderless does not mean that law does not apply. The chapter states that national and international regulations often apply where bitcoin touches traditional systems, and that exchanging bitcoin for national currency will often require proof of identity and banking information [AH]. The word also says nothing about prices, which float and can differ from market to market."),
 ]),
 ("Decentralized", "Decentralized", 3, "Characteristic", ("no single point of control", "Bitcoin is decentralized by design and free of any central authority or point of control that can be attacked or corrupted.", None), [
  (W, "A decentralized system is one that has no central authority or point of control. The chapter says that Bitcoin is decentralized by design, and free of any central authority or point of control that can be attacked or corrupted [AH]. Its ordinary statement of the same idea is that there is no central server or point of control in the system."),
  (Y, "Centralization is a weakness of the systems that came before. The sidebar explains that earlier digital currencies worked but were centralized and, as a result, were easy to attack by governments and hackers; they used a central clearinghouse to settle all transactions, and most were litigated out of existence or collapsed when their parent company liquidated abruptly [AH]. Decentralization removes the single party whose failure or coercion would end the system."),
  (E, "The property appears in the way that Bitcoin is run. No one owns the network; the chapter states that neither Satoshi Nakamoto nor anyone else exerts individual control over the Bitcoin system, which operates based on fully transparent mathematical principles, open source code and consensus among participants [AH]. Miners, nodes and wallet developers are separate parties."),
  (H, "Decentralization is achieved by spreading each function across many participants. Validation is done by every full node, issuance and clearing are done by miners competing for blocks, and the rules are written in software that everyone can read. The result is that no participant can unilaterally change a rule or block a payment; changing a rule requires nearly everyone to run new software."),
  (K, "Decentralization is a matter of degree and it can erode. If most of the mining were done by a few pools, or most users relied on a few custodians, the practical control would be concentrated, even though the design allows otherwise. The word also does not mean absence of rules: Bitcoin has strict rules, enforced by software instead of by a person."),
 ]),
 ("Robust", "Robust", 3, "Characteristic", ("resisting intervention", "A robust digital currency is built to withstand intervention by antagonists, whether legitimate governments or criminal elements, which a decentralized design makes possible.", None), [
  (W, "Robust means able to withstand attack and interference and to keep working while under them. The chapter's sidebar states the requirement in one sentence: to be robust against intervention by antagonists, whether legitimate governments or criminal elements, a decentralized digital currency was needed to avoid a single point of attack [AH]. The two words of that sentence that a student should notice are antagonists, which covers both lawful and unlawful opponents, and single point of attack, which names what the design removes rather than what it adds."),
  (Y, "The history of digital money supplies the motive. The earlier projects were targeted by worried governments and were eventually litigated out of existence, or failed in spectacular crashes when the parent company liquidated abruptly [AH]. A currency intended to last must not depend on a company or an institution that can be pressured, sued or closed."),
  (E, "Robustness is a property of the whole design. The chapter points to mining as a security mechanism, noting that the number and power of the machines running the proof-of-work algorithm have increased exponentially, and that their combined computational power now exceeds the combined computing operations of the world's top supercomputers [AH]. The more work that protects the record, the harder it is to attack."),
  (H, "Robustness follows from decentralization plus cost. Because there is no central point, an attacker must act against many independent participants. Because the record is protected by proof of work, rewriting it would require repeating the work. The whitepaper expresses the resulting guarantee: as long as honest nodes control the majority of the computing power, the honest chain will grow the fastest and outpace any competing chains [NK]."),
  (K, "Robust is not the same as invulnerable. The guarantee depends on the honest majority assumption, on the correctness of the software, and on users protecting their keys; the chapter itself lists dangers such as malware and phishing. A robust network can contain fragile wallets, and the weakest link is often the user's own device."),
 ]),
 ("Architecture", None, 2, "Nature", None, [
  (W, "The Architecture section describes how the Bitcoin system is put together. After the opening pages, the chapter lists what Bitcoin consists of: a decentralized peer-to-peer network, a public transaction journal, a set of rules for independent transaction validation and currency issuance, and a mechanism for reaching global decentralized consensus on the valid blockchain [AH]. It adds that Bitcoin builds on decades of research in cryptography and distributed systems and includes at least four key innovations brought together in a unique and powerful combination."),
  (Y, "The combination, rather than any single part, is the invention. Digital signatures, hash functions and peer-to-peer networks existed before. What was new was the arrangement in which they support each other, so that a money system works without a central party. A student who can name the four parts and say what each contributes understands the structure of the whole subject."),
  (H, "Three of the four parts are concepts of this section. The peer-to-peer protocol describes the network. The public journal describes the record. The consensus rule set describes the rules every participant applies. The fourth part, the proof-of-work mechanism, is explained in the Consensus section of the Network branch, because it is the means by which the other three are brought to agreement."),
 ], None),
 ("PeerToPeerProtocol", "Peer-to-peer protocol", 3, "Architecture", ("a decentralized peer-to-peer network", "The Bitcoin protocol defines a decentralized peer-to-peer network in which every full node is a peer that both provides and consumes services.", (_P2P, "4")), [
  (W, "The peer-to-peer protocol is the first of the four parts of Bitcoin that the chapter lists: a decentralized peer-to-peer network, which it identifies as the Bitcoin protocol [AH]. The textbook's chapter on the network explains that the term peer-to-peer means that the full nodes that participate are peers to each other, that they can all perform the same functions, and that there are no special nodes [AH]."),
  (Y, "A peer-to-peer design removes the server. With no central machine to run, pay for, defend or shut down, the network has no single point of failure or control. The network chapter observes that peer-to-peer networks are inherently resilient, decentralized and open, and that decentralization of control is a core design principle of Bitcoin that can only be achieved and maintained by a flat and decentralized network [AH]."),
  (E, "The network chapter describes the arrangement as a mesh with a flat topology, with no server, no centralized service and no hierarchy; nodes both provide and consume services at the same time. It also names file sharing as another large application of peer-to-peer technology [AH]. In the chapter's story, Joe's transaction reaches the whole network by being passed from peer to peer."),
  (H, "Each node connects to several others, and each message is passed on from node to node until it has reached everyone. The example models seven peers with a few links between them and floods a message from peer A; the program counts the rounds until every peer has it, and the answer is 4. The real network is far larger, but the principle is the same: reach grows with each round of forwarding."),
  (K, "Peer-to-peer does not mean that all participants are equal in practice. Many users run clients and not peers, as the chapter explains, and the network also contains gateway servers that extend it to other protocols [AH]. A message passed from peer to peer is also not instantly delivered everywhere: it takes a few seconds for a transaction to reach most well-connected nodes."),
 ]),
 ("PublicJournal", "Public journal", 3, "Architecture", ("the blockchain as a public transaction journal", "The second part of Bitcoin is a public transaction journal, the blockchain, in which every confirmed transaction is recorded for anyone to read.", None), [
  (W, "The public journal is the second part of Bitcoin in the chapter's list: a public transaction journal, the blockchain [AH]. A journal is a chronological record, and public means that anyone can read it. The chapter uses the same phrase when it explains confirmation, as the Bitcoin transaction journal known as the blockchain."),
  (Y, "A shared record is what lets strangers agree about who owns what without trusting one another. In a bank, only the bank sees the full ledger and the customers trust its statements. In Bitcoin the ledger is open, and every participant can check it, so trust is replaced by verification. The whitepaper makes the same argument: the only way to confirm the absence of a transaction is to be aware of all transactions, so the transactions must be publicly announced [NK]."),
  (E, "The journal is met in block explorers, in the data that nodes download, and in the way wallets compute balances. The chapter tells us that Alice's address first shows Joe's payment as unconfirmed and that it is confirmed when it is recorded in the journal [AH]. The privacy consequence is also public: the chapter notes that once a Bitcoin address is attached to an identity, associated transactions may become easy to identify and track."),
  (H, "The journal is stored as a chain of blocks, each of which commits to its predecessor, and every full node keeps a copy. New entries are added by miners, in blocks, about every ten minutes, and each node checks them before appending them to its copy. The Chain section of the Foundations branch explains the structure in detail."),
  (K, "Public does not mean that the entries carry names. They carry addresses and amounts, which is pseudonymous and not anonymous, as the privacy concept of the Usage branch explains, and the whitepaper describes the same arrangement as keeping the public keys anonymous so that an amount can be seen moving without anything linking it to a person [NK]. Nor does public mean complete: the journal holds only what was recorded on the chain, so payments made by the offchain methods of the Transfer group do not appear in it at all. A student should therefore read the journal as a record of onchain transfers between addresses and not as a list of who paid whom."),
 ]),
 ("ConsensusRuleSet", "Consensus rule set", 3, "Architecture", ("the rules every node applies", "The consensus rules are the set of rules for independent transaction validation and currency issuance that every participant applies in the same way.", (_RULE, "[True, False, True]")), [
  (W, "The consensus rule set is the third part of Bitcoin in the chapter's list: a set of rules for independent transaction validation and currency issuance, which the chapter calls consensus rules [AH]. They define what makes a transaction valid, what makes a block valid, and how many new bitcoins a block may create."),
  (Y, "Because Bitcoin has no referee, each participant must be able to decide for himself whether a transaction or a block is acceptable, and all of them must reach the same decision. The rules are the means of this. The chapter says that nobody can force you to accept any bitcoins that were created beyond the expected issuance rate [AH]; the rules make that statement true, because a block that breaks them is rejected."),
  (E, "The rules are written into the software of the nodes. Bitcoin Core's validation code, for instance, compares the amount that the first transaction of a block claims with the sum of the fees and the subsidy for the block height, and rejects the block if it claims more [BC]. The difficulty and subsidy rules are in the same code."),
  (H, "Each node checks every block and every transaction against the rules before it accepts it. The example is a miniature of the reward rule: a block at height 840000 may claim at most the subsidy of 312,500,000 satoshis plus the fees. A claim of exactly the subsidy is accepted, a claim one satoshi higher is rejected, and a claim that includes 12,500,000 satoshis of fees is accepted."),
  (K, "The rules are valid only as long as the participants run them. Changing a rule means persuading nearly everyone to run new software, and a minority that runs different rules ends up on a chain of its own. A rule is also different from a policy that a single node applies for convenience, such as the fee below which it will not relay a transaction."),
 ]),
